  "live_records": {
    "q-41702": {
      "dom_id": "step-q41702",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Consider the closed curve $C,$ which is the boundary of the region enclosed by $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and the $x$-axis, for $\\pi \\le x\\le 2\\pi .$ Express ${\\int }_{C}xdx-(x-y)dy$ as a repeated integral.",
      "worked_solution": "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14227492/assets/264cb888010525848f647669653d34383535dd32e6ac264927105540c3b85f9f.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=x$ and $Q(x,y)=-(x-y).$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):\\pi \\le x\\le 2\\pi ,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\le y\\le 0\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the Green's Theorem we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}xdx-(x-y)dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-1)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}^{0}dy\\,dx.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
